  "body": "From @isaacs in https://github.com/whatwg/streams/issues/13#issuecomment-37357941:\n\n> I think we ought to solve this problem empirically.  Here's what I'm imagining:\n> 1. Create a program that does this:\n>    \n>    ``` js\n>    readableStream.pipe(transformStream).pipe(writableStream)\n>    ```\n> 2. Make the following timings configurable:\n>    1. readableStream underlying source data rate\n>    2. transformStream passthrough speed (ie, how long a transform takes)\n>    3. transformStream size multiplier (ie, does data shrink or expand, maybe ranging from 0.01 to 100)\n>    4. writableStream consumption speed\n>    5. readableStream highWaterMark\n>    6. transformStream (readable side) highWaterMark\n>    7. transformStream (writable side) highWaterMark\n>    8. writableStream highWaterMark\n>    9. readableStream chunk write size\n> 3. For a range of values of a-i above, write a total data size of 16_1024_1024*1024 to the readableStream, and measure:\n>    1. time for writableStream to consume all the data\n>    2. total buffered data over time for each piece\n>    3. number of calls to the underlying readable source's start/stop mechanism\n> \n> A graph of these would be great.\n> \n> The benefit of writing such a test is that we'd be able to conclusively establish which sorts of approaches are better from an algorithmic pov, and then we could establish which API bikeshedding is even worth worrying about.\n",
